// Title: HackTool - Powerup Write Hijack DLL
// Sigma ID: 602a1f13-c640-4d73-b053-be9a2fa58b96
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Subhash Popuri (@pbssubhash)
// Date: 2021-08-21 | Modified: 2024-06-27
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, execution, stealth, T1574.001
// Description: Powerup tool's Write Hijack DLL exploits DLL hijacking for privilege escalation. In it's default
//   mode, it builds a self deleting .bat file which executes malicious command. The detection rule
//   relies on creation of the malicious bat file (debug.bat by default).
// False positives:
//   - Any powershell script that creates bat files
// References:
//   - https://powersploit.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Privesc/Write-HijackDll/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/file/file_event/file_event_win_hktl_powerup_dllhijacking.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where (InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\powershell.exe" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\pwsh.exe") and FolderPath endswith ".bat"
